using System.Net;
using FluentResults;
using MofonCode.Core;

namespace __SOLUTION__.Api;

/// <summary>
/// The body of every response this API sends.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>One shape for success and failure both, so a client writes one piece of code that reads it.
/// <c>Notices</c> is what a front end displays: it asks whether the call succeeded and, either way,
/// shows what the notices say. That is the whole contract — the front end does not interpret, map or
/// decide, and it certainly does not parse a message to work out what went wrong.</para>
///
/// <para><c>Data</c> is absent on a failure rather than present and empty, because an empty payload
/// beside <c>succeeded: false</c> invites a client to render it.</para>
/// </remarks>
/// <typeparam name="T">What the operation returns.</typeparam>
/// <param name="Succeeded">Whether the operation did what was asked.</param>
/// <param name="Data">What it produced, or null when it failed.</param>
/// <param name="Notices">What to tell the reader: errors on a failure, warnings worth saying on a success.</param>
/// <param name="TraceId">The id that connects this response to the log entry holding the detail.</param>
public sealed record ApiResponse<T>(
    bool Succeeded,
    T? Data,
    IReadOnlyList<Notice> Notices,
    string? TraceId);

/// <summary>
/// What every endpoint returns.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para><strong>This is the only <see cref="IResult"/> in the solution, deliberately.</strong>
/// <c>BannedSymbols.txt</c> bans <c>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Results</c> and <c>TypedResults</c>
/// outright, so there is no second way to build a response: a handler cannot reach for
/// <c>Results.Problem</c> and decide for itself what a reader is told. The ban is what makes this
/// type the boundary rather than a convention about where the boundary should be.</para>
///
/// <para>It writes the JSON itself rather than delegating to a framework factory, which is what lets
/// the ban be absolute. A sanctioned helper that called the banned API would need an exemption, and
/// the exemption is the hole every rule like this eventually leaks through.</para>
///
/// <para>The response is a property as well as a payload, so a test can assert what crosses without
/// standing up a server.</para>
/// </remarks>
/// <typeparam name="T">What the operation returns.</typeparam>
public sealed class ApiResult<T> : IResult
{
    internal ApiResult(ApiResponse<T> response, int statusCode)
    {
        Response = response;
        StatusCode = statusCode;
    }

    /// <summary>The body that will be written.</summary>
    public ApiResponse<T> Response { get; }

    /// <summary>The status code that will be set.</summary>
    public int StatusCode { get; }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public Task ExecuteAsync(HttpContext httpContext)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(httpContext);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = StatusCode;

        return httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(Response, httpContext.RequestAborted);
    }
}

/// <summary>
/// Turns a result into a response. The one conversion at the edge of this process.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>Every endpoint ends with this call and nothing else, which is what makes the rule about
/// failures enforceable rather than remembered: <c>ToEnvelope</c> drops every exceptional error and
/// everything caused by one, so a database exception's message — which names a server, a column, and
/// sometimes a credential — cannot reach a reader even when the code that failed knew it. The log
/// still gets all of it through <c>ToLogString</c>, which is the pair.</para>
///
/// <para>An architecture test asserts every endpoint returns <see cref="ApiResult{T}"/>, so an
/// endpoint that returned a domain type directly — and so sent a payload with no notices and no trace
/// id — fails the build rather than reaching a client.</para>
/// </remarks>
public static class Boundary
{
    /// <summary>Shapes a result for a reader outside this process.</summary>
    /// <typeparam name="T">What the operation returns.</typeparam>
    /// <param name="result">What the operation produced.</param>
    /// <param name="http">The request being answered, for its trace id.</param>
    /// <returns>The response, and the status code that goes with it.</returns>
    public static ApiResult<T> ToApiResult<T>(this Result<T> result, HttpContext http)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(result);
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(http);

        ResultEnvelope envelope = result.ToEnvelope(traceId: http.TraceIdentifier);

        return new ApiResult<T>(
            new ApiResponse<T>(
                envelope.Succeeded,
                result.IsSuccess ? result.Value : default,
                envelope.Notices,
                envelope.TraceId),
            StatusFor(result));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which status code a result deserves.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The one thing the envelope cannot carry. It has already replaced an exceptional error with a
    /// neutral sentence — which is the point — so by the time a client reads the body it cannot tell
    /// a bad request from a broken dependency. The two are different to whoever is calling: 400 says
    /// this will not work as sent, 500 says this was not your fault and retrying may.
    /// </remarks>
    private static int StatusFor<T>(Result<T> result)
    {
        if (result.IsSuccess)
        {
            return (int)HttpStatusCode.OK;
        }

        return result.HasError<IExceptionalError>()
            ? (int)HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError
            : (int)HttpStatusCode.BadRequest;
    }
}
